    "q-36834": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36834",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for \n $f(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2)-f(-1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{3}-{0}^{3})-({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-3{x}^{2},3{y}^{2}⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},3{c}_{2}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},3{c}_{2}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =-3{c}_{1}^{2}+3{c}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x+1}{0+1} \\\\ y-1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({c}_{2}-2)^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 4{c}_{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{1}={c}_{2}-2=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
