    "q-98118": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98118",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3,2)-f(0,1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨0,1,1⟩) \\\\ 3-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4z,-2y,4x+2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{3}-4{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2t+1,\\,z=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=2t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=t+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3=4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-4(2t+1)+6(t+1) & =3 \\\\ 4t-8t-4+6t+6 & =3 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})+1=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
